    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW27wdcZpy5tWFoUfsGvALeMr)_\n\nBefore: each of the 128 literate module docs in `docs/tangles/` explained how to tangle it, `.config/emacs-modular/README.md` was Markdown and said nothing about tangling, `docs/main.org` had no overview of the modules, and the dotfiles `README.org` didn't link to the Emacs README.\n\nAfter: `.config/emacs-modular/README.org` (converted from README.md) has a \"Tangling the literate files\" section that explains the `#+PROPERTY` file header, how to tangle one file, and a block (plus a fish/bash one-liner) that tangles all 128 module docs. Each doc's intro now points to its file header and to that section. `docs/main.org` has a \"Module tree\" section after the preamble that mirrors the `libraries/modules/` directories, linking each module's doc and `.el` file. The dotfiles `README.org` links to the Emacs README and to `docs/main.org`.\n\nHow: the doc intros were rewritten with a script (one line per file). No `.el` file changes. I ran the tangle-everything block on a copy of the repo with Emacs 29.3: it tangles 128 files and the only differences are the ones already documented (final newlines, the 5 empty modules getting their skeletons, trailing spaces in sp-org-archive.el). The other docs in `docs/tangles/` (`-old.org` files and older notes that tangle to `init.el`) are skipped because their headers don't point into `libraries/modules/`.\n\nThe dotfiles root README was already `README.org`, so it only gained the link.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_013V6WSXthgpc9jPWyZsevsf)_",
